        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best dumbbell set for a home gym?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most home gyms, REP Fitness's 5 to 50 lb rubber hex set with its three-tier rack, for its straight knurled handles, lifetime home warranty and compact rack. If cost matters most, CAP's 550 lb rubber hex set with a two-tier rack covers the same range for roughly $1.27 to $1.45 a pound on the prices we saw.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much does a full dumbbell set cost?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On 19 October 2026, a 5 to 50 lb set — ten pairs, 550 lb — cost about $700 to $800 from CAP with a rack, $1,100 to $1,310 from REP in rubber, and about $1,600 from REP in urethane before a rack. That works out at roughly $1.27 to $2.91 a pound. Smaller 100 and 150 lb starter sets with racks were about $150 to $260.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are rubber dumbbells or urethane dumbbells better?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Urethane is tougher, slower to show wear and usually smells less, which matters indoors; it also costs much more. Rubber is cheaper, quiet and kind to floors, but new rubber sets often smell for a while and can scuff or crack over years. For a garage, rubber is usually enough; for a bedroom or office, urethane is worth considering.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do rubber dumbbells smell?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Often, at first. Even dumbbells sold as low-odor can smell strongly out of the box, and many arrive with an oily protective coating. Unbox them somewhere ventilated, wipe them down and give them time before bringing them into a living space. If you are sensitive to odors and the set will live indoors, urethane is the safer choice.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a full dumbbell rack too heavy for an upstairs floor?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It may be. A 5 to 50 lb set on its rack is about 600 lb on a few square feet, well above the 40 lb per square foot minimum design live load the residential code uses for most rooms. That does not mean a floor will fail, but put a full rack on a ground floor or slab where you can, and ask a structural engineer or builder before putting one upstairs.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Was the Amazon Basics dumbbell recall for fixed dumbbells?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Amazon's May 2026 recall covers Amazon Basics 55 Lbs. Adjustable Dumbbells with lot code C250306VJAW5 on the outer box, sold from July to September 2025, because plates can fail to engage and dislodge. It does not cover Amazon Basics fixed rubber hex dumbbells. Owners of the recalled adjustable set should stop using it and follow the notice.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

